 ],
 "literal": "Nature-attained gooseberry's green unripe fruit, / fierce tiger's cub, as a toy, spinning, / the difficult-to-cross hills, we crossed; / short steps, many, we do not think of indeed; at the beginning of the path, / in the desert, thrived crooked-branched scarlet ixora's / bud-unfolded many flowers' fragrance-emitting / dark black hair-possessing young woman's friendship indeed.",
 "translation": "We have crossed the difficult hills where the naturally fallen green gooseberries spin as playthings for the cubs of the fierce tiger. Yet, we do not think of the many weary short steps we had to take, because our minds are filled with the friendship of the young woman with dark black hair, which is as fragrant as the many opening buds of the crooked-branched Veᅡci that thrives in the desert at the start of the path.",
 "modern": "இயற்கையாக ஁தர்ந்த தெல்லி மரத்தின் பசல் காய்கள், காடிய புலிக்குட்டிகள் விலைடாடி உருட்டு૥ இடமாக விலங்கு૥, கடப்பத்கரிய மலைகளைக் கடத்து வல்த நா૥, அந்த வழியில் நடந்த பல சிறிய அடிகளின் வருத்தத்தை நினைக்கவே மாட்டு૥; ஏனெனில், வழியின் தொடக்கத்தில் உள்ள காட்டில் தழைத்த, வளைந்த கிலைகளையுடைய வெட்சி மரத்தின் இதழ் அவிழ்த்த பல மலல்களின் நறுமண் க૥ழு૥, கரிிய கூந்தலையுடைய தலைவியின் நட்பையே நா૥ நினைத்துக் கொண்டிருக்கிறுா૥.",
 "explanation": "The hero is speaking to himself or his companion after crossing a difficult desert path to return to his beloved. He describes the harsh landscape where fierce tiger cubs play by rolling fallen green gooseberries. Despite the arduous journey, he declares that he does not feel the fatigue of the many short steps he had to take. This is because his mind is entirely occupied by the sweet memory of his beloved's love. Her dark hair is as fragrant as the freshly opened Veᅡci flowers growing at the entrance of the desert path, showing how her love sustains him through all hardships.",
